---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/forestry/foundational/forests-and-forestry">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the first lesson in Forestry — the profession of managing long-lived
    ecosystems for many values at once. You need nothing from earlier lessons: no biology
    beyond "trees grow from seeds", no economics beyond "different people want different
    things". What the lesson builds is the discipline's distinctive frame — a crop that
    takes decades to grow and is, at the same time, a habitat, a water system and a carbon
    warehouse.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>say what forestry is, and why its time horizon makes it unlike any other land use;</li>
    <li>describe the world's three great forest belts and the layered structure forests share;</li>
    <li>tell the story of forestry's founding idea — sustained yield — from 1713 timber crisis to today's certification schemes;</li>
    <li>map the five value rows of a forest and the stakeholders who hold claims on them;</li>
    <li>read the global forest-area curve critically, and explain why "net loss" hides the real story;</li>
    <li>name the working cycle of a forester and the three classic silvicultural systems.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit for the whole subject: whenever someone announces what a forest is
    <em>for</em>, ask which row of the ledger they are reading and whose claims they are
    counting. Forestry is the discipline that makes that choosing explicit.
  </p>

  <h2>The crop that takes eighty years</h2>
  <p>
    Forestry is the management of long-lived ecosystems for multiple, competing values:
    wood, water, wildlife, carbon and people. Unlike most land uses, the crop takes
    decades to grow — so a forester's decisions today describe a forest someone else will
    harvest. That time horizon, plus the fact that forests are habitats as much as
    resources, is what makes the discipline distinctive.
  </p>
  <p>
    The time horizon is worth dwelling on because it drives everything else in the
    profession. A wheat farmer can change crops next season; a forester who clears a stand
    of 80-year oaks cannot buy 80-year oaks for the next 80 years. Every decision is
    therefore a loan against the future, and the discipline's founding question — <em>how
    much can be taken, how often, without diminishing the source?</em> — is really an
    intergenerational contract. The second distinctive feature follows from the first:
    because the crop is simultaneously an ecosystem, every harvest is also a habitat
    decision, a water decision and a carbon decision. Forestry is where ecology and
    economics are forced to share a spreadsheet, and this lesson walks both sides of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> long rotations plus multiple values make forestry a
    negotiation with the future. Before we can follow the negotiation, we need to know
    what is being negotiated over — what a forest actually is.
  </p>

  <h2>What forests are</h2>
  <p>
    Formally, a forest is land dominated by trees — the FAO's working definition asks for
    tree canopy over 10% of the ground and an area above half a hectare — but the formal
    definition undersells the variety. The world's forests sort into three great belts.
    <strong>Boreal</strong> forest rings the subarctic in a belt of pine, spruce, fir and
    birch across Scandinavia, Russia, Alaska and Canada: slow-growing, fire-shaped,
    covering huge continuous areas — this conifer belt holds roughly a third of all the
    trees on Earth. Its trees are engineered for short seasons: dark needles absorb the
    weak, low-angle sun; keeping needles year-round gives a head start on spring growth
    instead of rebuilding foliage each year; and the conical canopy sheds snow. Soils
    beneath it are poor and acidic, so the forest recycles its nutrients tightly through a
    mat of undecayed litter and fungal partners on the roots.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="file each fact under its belt" widget="sort-buckets" config={ffBeltsSort} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> File each fact under its belt and watch the buckets' personalities emerge: boreal holds
    roughly a third of all trees across Scandinavia, Russia, Alaska and Canada; temperate
    owns the richest soils and the wood industries' historic centres; tropical holds most of
    the biodiversity and carbon. Notice the FAO footnote in the widget — canopy over 10 per
    cent of the ground and half a hectare makes a forest, however much the belts differ.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="pair each boreal trait with its job" widget="match" config={ffBorealMatch} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Pair each boreal trait with its job and read the belt as an engineering brief: dark needles
    absorb the weak, low-angle sun, year-round needles buy a head start on spring growth,
    and the conical canopy sheds snow. Notice the soil card's twist — poor, acidic ground is
    answered by tight recycling through a mat of undecayed litter and fungal partners on the
    roots.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Temperate</strong> forests — the deciduous and mixed woods of Europe, eastern
    North America and East Asia, plus the conifer rainforests of the Pacific Northwest —
    grow on the richest soils and carry the wood industries' historic centres.
    <strong>Tropical</strong> forests — the humid rainforests of Amazonia, the Congo basin
    and Southeast Asia, plus the seasonally dry woodlands — hold most of the world's
    forest biodiversity and carbon. Their architecture is vertical: solitary emergent
    giants push above 50 metres, the continuous canopy below shades the floor so deeply
    that undergrowth is often surprisingly sparse, and woody lianas and epiphytic plants
    climb the scaffolding to reach the light. Competition is so fierce that a seedling may
    wait decades for a light gap. And the soil is poorer than the luxuriance suggests:
    heavy rain leaches tropical soils for centuries, so most of the ecosystem's nutrients
    are locked in the living biomass itself, not the ground beneath it — a fact with harsh
    consequences that returns below.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="call the emergents' height" widget="estimate" config={ffEmergentEstimate} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Estimate before revealing: the emergent giants break clean above the canopy at over 50
    metres. Notice what the height means for the forest below: a floor shaded so deeply that
    undergrowth stays surprisingly sparse, while woody lianas and epiphytes climb the
    scaffolding for the light a seedling may wait decades to find.
  </p>
  <p>
    Structure matters more than species lists: every forest is layered — canopy,
    understorey, floor, soil — and those layers are distinct habitats; many birds and
    beetles never leave one stratum. Two functional facts define what a forest <em>does</em>.
    It is the largest living carbon store on land: trees and forest soils together hold
    hundreds of billions of tonnes of carbon, a pool on the same order of magnitude as the
    atmosphere's own. And it is a water machine: canopies intercept rain, roots open the
    soil, and a forested catchment yields steadier, cleaner flow than any other land
    cover. So the answer to "what is a forest?" is three answers: a biological community,
    a carbon warehouse and a hydrological instrument.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a plantation of even-aged pines is "not a forest". By
  definition it is — and the argument it provokes is the discipline's central one in
  miniature: it stores carbon and produces fibre efficiently, but supports far less
  biodiversity and (with dark, dense, single-species canopy) often less water regulation
  than a natural stand. Whether a plantation is good forestry depends entirely on what it
  replaced and what it is managed for. The honest unit of judgement is the value ledger,
  below, not the tree count.</p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="the plantation verdict, argued properly" widget="branch" config={ffPlantationBranch} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Branch the argument and resist both easy answers: by the FAO definition the pine plantation
    is a forest, and being a forest settles nothing — it stores carbon and grows fibre
    efficiently while supporting far less biodiversity. Notice where the walk lands: the
    verdict hangs on what stood before and what the stand is for.
  </p>

  <h2>A short history of forestry: from alarm to science</h2>
  <p>
    Modern forestry began in 18th-century Germany with a simple accounting idea: harvest
    no more wood per year than the forest grows. Sustained yield made forestry a science
    of rotations — plant, tend, harvest, regenerate — and later broadened into sustained
    yield of <em>everything</em> a forest provides. Its modern descendants are
    certification schemes and multi-criteria management plans.
  </p>
  <p>
    The idea arrived as an emergency response. By the early 1700s, German mining districts
    faced a genuine <em>Holznot</em> — timber shortage — because shafts, smelters and
    salt-boiling consumed forests faster than unmanaged woodland regenerated. Hans Carl
    von Carlowitz's <em>Sylvicultura oeconomica</em> (1713) answered with the principle of
    <strong>nachhaltende Nutzung</strong> — sustainable use — the first written statement
    that harvest must respect regrowth. Georg Ludwig Hartig and the forest academies
    turned it into mathematics (the <em>normal forest</em>: age classes in equal areas,
    yielding an even annual cut), and the German model was exported worldwide — to
    British India (forest departments from the 1860s), the United States (the Forest
    Service, 1905, under Gifford Pinchot, trained at Nancy and shaped by German practice),
    and beyond. The export carried a politics: European state forestry often criminalised
    long-established local uses — common grazing, gathering, slash-and-burn — and the
    resulting conflicts shaped forest policy debates that persist in every former colony.
    The twentieth century broadened the goal from sustained yield of <em>timber</em> to
    <em>multiple use</em> (the US Multiple-Use Sustained-Yield Act of 1960) and then to
    <em>ecosystem management</em>; the twenty-first added certification (FSC, 1993; PEFC)
    and carbon accounting. History's arc: alarm → accounting → science → negotiation —
    and the negotiation stage is where forestry now lives.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="line up the sustained-yield arc" widget="order" config={ffSustainedOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Order the founding idea's career and read the arc: Carlowitz's 1713 answer to a genuine
    timber shortage, Hartig's normal forest turning it into mathematics, the export to
    British India's forest departments and the US Forest Service, multiple use in 1960, and
    certification with carbon accounting from 1993. Notice the widget's summary of the whole
    line — alarm, accounting, science, negotiation — the stage where forestry now lives.
  </p>

  <h2>Forest values and stakeholders: the ledger</h2>
  <p>
    Ask what a forest provides and you get the discipline's value ledger — five rows that
    every management plan must weigh together.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="what forests provide — the value ledger" widget="equationStep" config={valuesCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the rows are not a menu — a single stand of trees
    produces all five at once, and improving one row can quietly degrade another. That is
    the whole management problem in miniature: the rows trade against each other, so
    someone must decide the weighting, and the decision is visible to everyone it affects.
  </p>
  <p>
    The five ledger rows compete, and the competition is the profession's daily work. Wood
    and fibre remain the largest material harvest — roughly <strong>2 billion m³ of
    industrial roundwood a year</strong>, plus even more fuelwood globally — and wood's
    green credentials are real but conditional: as a material it stores carbon and
    substitutes for steel and concrete, but only regrowth (or clearly growing stocks)
    earns that credit. Water regulation is why so many great cities — New York's Catskill
    watersheds is the textbook case — drink from managed or protected forests: filtration
    for free, provided the catchment stays wooded. Biodiversity habitat makes every
    forestry decision a conservation decision: forests hold the majority of terrestrial
    species, and the difference between a clearcut, a shelterwood and a selection system
    is, to a beetle or a warbler, the difference between catastrophe, compromise and
    continuity. Climate services cut both ways — standing forest stores carbon while
    deforestation (roughly 10–12% of anthropogenic emissions in recent accounting years,
    with land-use change overall) transfers it back — and livelihoods and culture are the
    row economists struggle to price: hundreds of millions of people, many of them
    Indigenous or rural poor, live in or from forests.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="true or false — reading the ledger rows" widget="judge" config={ffLedgerJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Flip the four cards and mark the conditional one: harvesting does not automatically earn
    climate credit — only regrowth does, since wood substitutes for steel and concrete but
    conditionally. Notice the true cards' spread too: New York drinking from protected
    watersheds, the beetle's-eye difference between clearcut, shelterwood and selection, and
    deforestation's roughly 10–12 per cent of emissions.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <strong>stakeholders</strong> are the ledger's claimants, and mapping them is the
    first step of any real management plan: timber companies and forest workers (income,
    jobs); local and Indigenous communities (foods, medicines, grazing, sacred sites —
    and, under instruments like ILO Convention 169 and FSC's rules, consent rights);
    national and local governments (tax, revenue, watershed protection, carbon
    commitments); recreation and tourism users; conservation NGOs and future generations
    (whose claims are held by the others); and the global public, newly a stakeholder
    through climate policy (REDD+ — paying tropical countries to keep forests standing —
    is the global public literally entering the ledger). The stakeholder map explains
    forestry's chronic controversies (old-growth logging, bioenergy, plantations on
    community land): each is a dispute about which ledger row weighs most — and the
    discipline's mature position is that the rows are not independently satisfiable, so
    the choice must be explicit, documented and revisited.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> So far: the value ledger has five competing rows — wood, water, biodiversity, climate,
    livelihoods — and a map of claimants from timber companies and Indigenous communities to
    the global public, whose REDD+ payments literally enter the accounts; since no weighting
    satisfies every row, choosing one is the profession's daily work.
  </p>

  <h2>The global picture: stabilising but uneven</h2>
  <p>
    The world's forest area has stopped shrinking overall — FAO's assessments show the
    decline slowing from over 10 million hectares lost per year in the 1990s to a small
    residual net loss, with gains in temperate regions offsetting losses in the tropics.
    The global number hides the story: primary tropical forest keeps falling while
    plantations and regrowth rise. Watch that tension in the curve below.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="global forest area, 1990–2020 (FAO FRA)" widget="plotter" config={faoCfg} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the curve flattens across the three decades — the
    steep fall of the 1990s eases to nearly level by the 2010s. But a single global line
    is a sum of two very different movements, and the flattening is a net, not a peace.
    Read it against its geography: about 4.06 billion hectares in 2020, some 31% of land
    area, is the net of temperate and boreal area <em>rising</em> (farmland abandonment,
    plantations, fire suppression) and tropical forest <em>falling</em>, driven
    overwhelmingly by agriculture — pasture for cattle in Amazonia, smallholder and
    plantation crops elsewhere. The netting is exactly why the headline number needs the
    primary-forest and degradation data alongside it: a hectare of new plantation does
    not replace a hectare of primary rainforest in carbon, biodiversity or water
    regulation, any more than a new suburb replaces a cathedral. FAO's own reporting
    distinguishes naturally regenerating from planted forest for precisely this reason.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> planting trees is automatically good for the climate,
  so the tonnage matters more than the place. The slogan feels self-evidently true — trees
  absorb carbon, so more trees must mean less carbon. But where and how planting happens
  decides nearly everything: afforesting natural grasslands or peatlands can release more
  carbon than it stores and destroy existing habitat, while restoring degraded forest
  land is among the best climate instruments available — and a monoculture plantation and
  a restored mixed forest can carry identical "trees planted" counts while sitting at
  opposite ends of the value ledger.</p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="drag the divider — slogan vs ledger" widget="compare" config={ffPlantingCompare} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Drag the divider and watch the slogan meet the ledger: 'trees absorb carbon, so more trees
    must mean less carbon' runs into the finding that afforesting natural grasslands or
    peatlands can release more carbon than it stores. Notice what identical tree counts can
    hide — a monoculture plantation and a restored mixed forest sit at opposite ends of the
    value ledger, so where and how decides nearly everything.
  </p>

  <h2>What foresters actually do</h2>
  <p>
    The working cycle is: know the trees (identification and <em>silvics</em> — each
    species' tolerance of shade, fire, frost and competition, the knowledge that decides
    what can regenerate where); measure the forest (<em>mensuration</em>: plot-based
    inventory of species, diameter, height and volume; the increment core that reads age
    and growth from rings); decide the treatment (<em>silviculture</em>); plan across
    decades (management plans with rotations, allowable cuts and habitat objectives); and
    defend the result (<em>protection</em> from fire, pests and disease).
  </p>
  <p>
    Silvicultural systems are the craft's core vocabulary: <em>clearcutting</em> (all
    trees removed — suits shade-intolerant, even-aged species like pines, at maximum
    ecological cost), <em>shelterwood</em> (seed trees left through regeneration, then
    removed), and <em>selection</em> (individual trees or small groups harvested, keeping
    continuous uneven-aged cover — the near-natural system, demanding the most skill).
    Behind all of it stands ecology — because a managed forest is still an ecosystem, run
    on sunlight and soil.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="find the faulty line — three cutting methods" widget="spot-error" config={ffCuttingSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> Find the faulty line: selection — harvesting individual trees or small groups under
    continuous uneven-aged cover — demands the most skill of the three systems, not the
    least. Notice the trio's logic: clearcutting suits shade-intolerant even-aged pines at
    maximum ecological cost, shelterwood leaves seed trees standing through regeneration,
    and the near-natural option asks the most of the forester.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> forestry is mostly about cutting trees. The image is
  understandable — the chainsaw is the only part most people ever see. But the harvest is
  the shortest and least frequent operation in the cycle; the profession's daily work is
  regeneration (choosing what the next forest will be), tending (thinnings that shape
  decades of growth), protection and measurement. The harvest is the punctuation, and the
  sentences between are silviculture.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Why can a forester not "change crops next season" the way a farmer can — and what does that imply for decisions?</li>
    <li>Tropical rainforest soils are often exhausted within a few years of clearing. Where were the nutrients before the clearing?</li>
    <li>A city drinks from a forested watershed. Which ledger row is doing the work, and what happens if the catchment is cleared?</li>
    <li>The global forest-area curve flattens after 2015. Why does that not mean tropical deforestation has stopped?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) The rotation is decades: 80-year oaks cannot be bought
    for 80 years, so every decision is a loan against a future the decision-maker will not
    see — hence plans, allowable cuts and the habit of treating harvest as an
    intergenerational contract. (2) Mostly in the living biomass and surface litter, not
    the mineral soil — centuries of rain have leached tropical soils, so clearing the
    trees removes the nutrient bank, and what fertility remains is quickly lost to leaching
    and erosion. (3) Water regulation: canopies slow rainfall, soils absorb and filter it.
    Cleared catchments shed water faster and dirtier — filtration would have to be rebuilt
    with works and budgets, which is why protecting the watershed was cheaper than any
    treatment plant. (4) The flattening is a net: temperate gains (abandonment,
    plantations) cancel tropical losses, and a planted hectare does not replace a primary
    hectare in carbon, biodiversity or water regulation — so the net line must be read
    with primary-forest and degradation data.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Forestry manages long-lived ecosystems for competing values; its crop takes decades, so every decision is a loan against the future.</li>
    <li>Three great belts — boreal, temperate, tropical — differ in species, soils and architecture, but all are layered habitats.</li>
    <li>A forest is three things at once: biological community, carbon warehouse, hydrological instrument.</li>
    <li>Sustained yield (Carlowitz, 1713) is the founding idea: harvest must respect regrowth; the goal has since broadened from timber to ecosystems.</li>
    <li>The value ledger — wood, water, biodiversity, climate, livelihoods — has claimants (stakeholders) whose claims cannot all be maximised at once.</li>
    <li>Global forest area is a net of rising temperate and falling tropical forest; plantations do not substitute for primary forest.</li>
    <li>The forester's cycle: know, measure, treat, plan, protect — harvest is punctuation, silviculture is the sentence.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson gave the frame; the rest of the unit goes inside it. Coming next are the
    pieces of the working cycle in order — how forests regenerate and how foresters choose
    among silvicultural systems, how mensuration turns a stand of trees into numbers a
    plan can use, and how protection (fire, pests) is managed in an age when the old
    answers are failing. If the ledger idea stuck, you will find yourself using it
    throughout: every technique that follows is a way of moving one row of the ledger
    while keeping an honest account of the others.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three harder topics show where the discipline is heading. First, <strong>fire as
    management problem</strong>: a century of aggressive suppression in fire-adapted
    western US and Australian forests built fuel loads that now drive catastrophic fires —
    the policy correction (prescribed burning, thinning, letting low-intensity fire do its
    ecological work) is one of forestry's hardest re-learnings, because it means
    <em>using</em> the disturbance the profession was built to exclude.
  </p>
  <p>
    Second, <strong>the carbon arithmetic</strong>: wood in long-lived products stores
    carbon, but only decades of regrowth repay a harvest's initial carbon debt; this is
    why carbon accounting distinguishes storage in forest, storage in products and
    substitution effects — and why "wood is climate-neutral" is true only under specific,
    checkable assumptions (the 2010s debates over counting forest bioenergy as
    carbon-neutral made the subtlety famous).
  </p>
  <p>
    Third, <strong>ownership as destiny</strong>: who owns a forest predicts its
    management better than its ecology does — public forests, industrial private,
    smallholder and community forests respond to different incentives, and the global
    experiment (China's collective forest reforms, Latin America's Indigenous titling,
    which studies associate with lower deforestation) suggests community tenure is among
    the most effective conservation instruments known.
  </p>
  <p>
    The through-line for the whole unit: forestry's founding insight — that a harvest
    must respect a growth rate — has scaled from Carlowitz's mine timbers to planetary
    carbon, and every generation of foresters has had to renegotiate what, exactly, is
    being sustained, and for whom.
  </p>

